  tldr={[
    'Vague goals like "do better" fail because you cannot act on them. <strong>Specific, measurable</strong> goals turn into tasks.',
    'Set <strong>process goals</strong> (what you will do) as well as outcome goals (the grade). You control the first, not the second.',
    'Hard-but-realistic goals drive more effort than easy ones, as long as you are <strong>committed</strong> to them.',
    'Review goals weekly and adjust. A goal you never check is a wish.',
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'why', label: 'Why goals change behaviour' },
    { id: 'smart', label: 'The SMART formula' },
    { id: 'process', label: 'Process vs outcome goals' },
    { id: 'track', label: 'Track and adjust' },
    { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
  ]}
  tools={['/tools/study-plan-generator/', '/tools/gpa-calculator/', '/tools/exam-countdown/']}
  faqs={[
    { q: 'What are SMART study goals?', a: 'Goals that are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. For example, "score 80% on the next chemistry test by finishing all past papers two weeks before" instead of "get better at chemistry".' },
    { q: 'Should I set grade goals or effort goals?', a: 'Both, but lead with effort. You control how many past papers you do, not the exact mark. Process goals keep you moving even when an outcome feels far away or uncertain.' },
    { q: 'How many study goals should I have at once?', a: 'A few at most. Too many goals split your focus and none get done. Pick one or two outcome goals per term and a handful of weekly process goals to feed them.' },
