    { text: 'Locke, E. A., & Latham, G. P. (2002). Building a practically useful theory of goal setting and task motivation. American Psychologist, 57(9), 705–717.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.57.9.705' },
    { text: 'Zimmerman, B. J. (2002). Becoming a self-regulated learner: an overview. Theory Into Practice, 41(2), 64–70.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1207/s15430421tip4102_2' },
    { text: 'Gollwitzer, P. M. (1999). Implementation intentions: strong effects of simple plans. American Psychologist, 54(7), 493–503.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.54.7.493' },
  ]}
>
  <p>"I want to do well this year" is not a goal, it is a mood. It gives you nothing to do on a Tuesday afternoon. The point of a goal is to decide the behaviour, and the research on goal-setting is one of the most solid findings in psychology.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why goals change behaviour</h2>
  <p>Across hundreds of studies, Locke and Latham found that specific, challenging goals produce higher performance than vague or easy ones, provided the person is committed and gets feedback. Goals work by directing attention, raising effort, and helping you persist. "Try your best" loses to "finish these ten questions" almost every time.</p>

  <h2 id="smart">The SMART formula</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Specific:</strong> name the subject, task and target.</li>
    <li><strong>Measurable:</strong> a number you can check, such as a score or a count of papers.</li>
    <li><strong>Achievable:</strong> a stretch, not a fantasy.</li>
    <li><strong>Relevant:</strong> tied to something that actually matters to you.</li>
    <li><strong>Time-bound:</strong> a deadline, usually your exam date.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Anchor the deadline with the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a>, and use the <a href="/tools/gpa-calculator/">GPA calculator</a> to see which grades move your average, so your goals aim where it counts.</p>

  <h2 id="process">Process vs outcome goals</h2>
  <p>An outcome goal is the result ("80% in the final"). A process goal is the behaviour that gets you there ("three past papers a week"). You control the process, not the outcome, so lead with process goals and let them drive the result. Zimmerman's self-regulated learning work shows strong students cycle between setting a goal, acting, and judging progress, over and over.</p>

  <h2 id="track">Track and adjust</h2>
  <p>Turn each weekly process goal into dated blocks with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a>, and phrase them as if-then plans ("after dinner on Monday, one past paper") to lift follow-through, per Gollwitzer. Review every week: did you hit it, and if not, was the goal wrong or the plan? Adjust and continue. For turning goals into a term-long plan, see <a href="/guides/how-to-make-a-semester-study-plan/">how to make a semester study plan</a>.</p>
</Guide>
